# Copy this file to `.env` at the repo root and adjust the values for your
# machine. `.env` is gitignored — every developer/environment keeps their own.
#
# One file, two prefixes: each Runtime keeps the prefix its bundler inlines
# (ADR-0003). Nothing maps one group onto the other — a value shared by both
# Runtimes is spelled twice, on purpose.
#
# The `*_BASE_DOMAIN` values below are the origin an app is BUILT against — the
# URL it knows itself by. They are not the port a dev server binds. Each app
# declares its own two listening ports in `apps/<app>/ports.env`, and nowhere
# else: `_template_vite` on 3000 (dev) / 3100 (e2e), `_template_next` on 3001 /
# 3101. `bun run gen:app` assigns a new app the lowest free pair — dev 3000+n,
# e2e 3100+n — by scanning `apps/*` and writing that app's own `ports.env`.
# (`apps/storybook` sits outside both bands, on Storybook's own 6006.) So moving
# a port is an edit in `ports.env`; whether a value here has to follow is a
# separate question about which origin the bundle should call.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUBLIC_* — read by a Vite app through `import.meta.env`, validated by
# `@monorepo/env/vite/*` (`createEnv(baseEnvSchema, import.meta.env)`).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PUBLIC_APP_ENV=local
PUBLIC_BASE_DOMAIN=http://localhost:3000
PUBLIC_BASE_DOMAIN_API=http://localhost:8000

# Declared by apps/documents/src/env.ts, where every primitive page builds its
# demo link from it. Required rather than optional on purpose: a missing value
# should fail the image build, named, instead of shipping 63 pages whose demo
# link goes nowhere. Locally this is the Storybook dev server (`bun run
# dev:storybook`); a deployed environment points it at the published Storybook.
# A value that belongs to ONE app carries that app's name, because this file is
# shared by every app — see packages/env/README.md.
PUBLIC_DOCUMENTS_STORYBOOK_URL=http://localhost:6006

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* — read by a Next app through `@monorepo/env/next/*` (t3-env).
# `next dev`/`next build` only reads a `.env` inside the app directory, so the
# app's own scripts load this one with `dotenv -e ../../.env --` first.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV=local
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_DOMAIN=http://localhost:3001
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_DOMAIN_API=http://localhost:8000

# Sentry project DSN. Empty means the SDK stays installed but disabled — no
# network, no console noise (see packages/sentry/src/options.ts). Both the
# browser and the server SDK read this one inlined value, so it carries the
# client prefix even though the server uses it too.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# Sentry DSN for the portfolio project only (org `sentry`, project
# `portfolio_v1`). App-specific values carry the app's name so two apps sharing
# this one root `.env` never fight over a key; `NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN` above
# stays the Template's. Empty means the SDK stays installed but disabled.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTFOLIO_SENTRY_DSN=

# The origin the portfolio site knows itself by — what `metadataBase`,
# `app/robots.ts` and `app/sitemap.ts` build absolute URLs from. Required, not
# optional: a sitemap with relative URLs is not a sitemap. It is a second key
# rather than a reuse of `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_DOMAIN`, because that one is shared
# by every Next app in this file and already names the Template's port.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTFOLIO_BASE_DOMAIN=http://localhost:3002

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-only variables carry NO prefix — Next keeps them out of the browser
# bundle and t3-env reads them straight from `process.env`. An app declares its
# own under `server` in its `env.ts`, and adds it here with a dev value (see
# packages/env/README.md).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Declared by apps/_template_next/src/env.ts, and `.optional()` there because
# the template has no backend to authenticate against — so it stays commented
# out and a fresh clone still builds. A real app drops the `.optional()` and
# fills this in.
# TEMPLATE_API_TOKEN=

# Sentry DSN for the mcp-weather project only. Empty means the SDK stays
# installed but disabled. App-specific values carry the app's name so two apps
# sharing this one root `.env` never fight over a key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MCP_WEATHER_SENTRY_DSN=

# The OpenWeatherMap key apps/mcp-weather signs `get-weather` and `get-forecast`
# with — a server-only secret, so no prefix. **Required**, not optional: those
# two MCP tools are the whole app, so a missing value must fail `next build` (and
# the Dockerfile's `import './src/env.ts'` step) by name rather than ship a
# server whose weather tools answer 401. The placeholder below is what keeps a
# bare `docker build` and CI green; get a real key at
# https://openweathermap.org/api and put it in your own root `.env`.
MCP_WEATHER_OPENWEATHERMAP_API_KEY=replace-with-your-openweathermap-key

# Sentry DSN for the assistant-ai project only. Empty means the SDK stays
# installed but disabled. App-specific values carry the app's name so two apps
# sharing this one root `.env` never fight over a key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSISTANT_AI_SENTRY_DSN=

# The Gemini key apps/assistant-ai signs every chat turn with — a server-only
# secret, so no prefix. It keeps the bare name `@ai-sdk/google` documents rather
# than an app-named one: the name belongs to the provider, and a second app
# wanting Gemini wants the same Google project key. **Required**, not optional: a
# chat app that cannot reach a model is broken, not degraded, so a missing value
# must fail `next build` (and the Dockerfile's `import './src/env.ts'` step) by
# name. The placeholder below is what keeps a bare `docker build` and CI green;
# get a real key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey and put it in your own
# root `.env`.
GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=replace-with-your-gemini-key

# Origin of the MCP server whose tools apps/assistant-ai lets the model call —
# apps/mcp-weather, which listens on 3004 in dev (see its `ports.env`). Optional:
# unset, the app is a plain Gemini chat instead of failing to boot, which is what
# a developer who is not running the weather server should get. App-named, unlike
# the Gemini key above: "the MCP server" is a name this repo invented, so a second
# app pointing at a different one would otherwise overwrite this value.
ASSISTANT_AI_MCP_DOMAIN=http://localhost:3004
